<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hinton St Mary Mosaic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hinton St Mary Mosaic is a fourth-century Roman floor mosaic found in 1963 at Hinton St Mary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Its central roundel shows the head of a man in front of the chi-rho monogram, widely thought to be one of the earliest known images of Christ. It is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hinton-st-mary-mosaic.jpg" alt="Hinton St Mary Mosaic" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fransars · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic2_-_plw.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The mosaic is large and almost complete, mostly red, yellow and cream, and covered two rooms joined by a small decorated threshold. On stylistic grounds it is dated to the 4th century and attributed to the mosaic workshop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorchester-dorset">Durnovaria</a>, modern Dorchester. The panel of the larger room, about 5.2 by 4.6 m, has at its centre the bust of a man, perhaps wearing a pallium, before a chi-rho flanked by two pomegranates. He is generally identified as Christ, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Emperor Constantine I</a> has also been suggested.</p><p>Around the central figure are four lunettes of hunting scenes, mostly a dog and a deer, and in the corners four busts that may stand for the winds or the seasons. The smaller room's panel shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellerophon">Bellerophon</a> killing the Chimera, a scene from Roman mythology that has been read in a Christian sense as good defeating evil, flanked by two further panels of dogs hunting deer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The local blacksmith, Walter John White, found the mosaic on 12 September 1963. The Dorset County Museum cleared it with the help of local people, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> lifted it for preservation. The room was long assumed to belong to a villa, its layout resembling a Roman dining room, but excavations in 2021 and 2022 led archaeologists to abandon that view; nothing found there dates from before about 270, and a second mosaic came to light in 2022.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The mosaic was laid on a floor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> from 1965. In 1997, ahead of the building work for the new roof over the museum's courtyard, staff levered it up and broke it into pieces, which were stored in boxes, with only the central portrait shown in the galleries. The Association for the Study and Preservation of Roman Mosaics protested and petitioned for the whole pavement to be displayed again, or lent to a museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>.</p><p>On 2 August 2019 villagers of Hinton St Mary were told that the mosaic would be partly returned to the Dorset County Museum, though the original head of Christ would not come back: it was to be lent to museums worldwide, and the Dorset museum would receive a replica. The mosaic was chosen as Object 44 in the BBC Radio 4 programme A History of the World in 100 Objects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hinton St Mary Mosaic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christianity spread from the 3rd century, and the <b>Hinton St Mary</b> mosaic shows an early image of Christ.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
